 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186520497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1045 "Sketching the Derivative of a Function From the Function's Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "752a2e8d-dfc4-5210-8dfe-31f383092e9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101483 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/17/174178e1d4c527bb81ea3ba726e5ef753b40dd85b452d65faf05590e7a387a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "986f6f9d-dbf1-5fb1-85d5-0cfcc16f723b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101483 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $\\text{const.}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from negative to $0$) when we pass through $x=4$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=4$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/877fcf017ab91010648de9a375e535ec7697841c8e317196c5759df6aca32e19.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1061 1224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "beaa6f10-10cb-5300-9425-d5fce50a2dec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1061 1225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ebc76df-237a-5efb-96f2-b3585e2b318b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1061 1229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe6a977a-c724-5c50-9d38-b0a7dc613ae3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1061 25278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "538a0daa-f570-5287-b6c1-2fd7946ab4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1061 25279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "826cab52-c133-5e02-bcc7-a04d9190cd1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x),$ as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and an inflection point at $x=0.5.$\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)?$\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5.$ So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1065 641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "732e85fe-aec5-51f6-806c-68e476077346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1045 "Sketching the Derivative of a Function From the Function's Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186520536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519987 #edb/ref 105