#edb/ref 17592186520224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520415 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9efc5b3d-9312-5242-bda0-ac0535473480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520436 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e5af535f-d80a-5c38-9d0e-a84eb6995c15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520460 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/67/67cfcaae560edcf2fda8c69770088113b6035b46c9951f67ef81f13b8ea9615e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3aba7b06-41e0-5e22-b2ba-cc3cc44b0081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520460 #edb/ref 1074 "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ \n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(-0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(0.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5$ are positive and negative, respectively.  \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7791dde88c3f8500047c0ebeb4553055d76a98c0c46f903816f41c1506d6a6a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520481 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/42e163dd119aaea37d2a373310b408a409023400a6f84ff520c464c6acb153b9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "862b92b4-3ae1-5d18-841c-0f1c9c5cb6ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520481 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0.3)$ | $0.3$ | $(0.3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈-0.3.$ So ${f}^{″}(-0.3)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(-0.3)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈-0.3,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1b048d1b6c430f023c0419d7c8442740b76b5d7e646f32fc870be9225649b70.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520488 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fe/fe369706a9e211bfc5265041bbc5d2ec0fab69eb205c23111f548785a2f88708.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a12bbd8d-9dfc-5edb-a067-bd2603dd76bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520488 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈0.$ So ${f}^{″}(0)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(0)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈0,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f5a0aa83f9348cfae1bf2ef472a00e1da51137d03284bc78eea67d929aaa80e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/53/53693867d697a4aaf7bedd2267ac2e77087b01a270f44ce68e9a4d303b81a5ad.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5bdd27b-0119-529a-86ed-c9eae1777103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520497 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to negative)  when we pass through $x=0$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=0$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3b558331bf2e816cf0df6d756f55686aeb06f6ec9ce496019bd06d14684b32.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520536 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186520613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2960f11d-8f32-50a8-8883-ed0e4c737260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186520481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520546 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186520552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520546 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b56d5e6-9f66-5459-b308-041c603898f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520546 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186520497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520552 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "522c7806-eb48-5c75-b4c5-3c468ee4a292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520552 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186520460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186520546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54ea61c8-4bc1-5ef1-9f92-18ea362bb2b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186520488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186520553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2e6d779-f0e8-5097-a067-f233c8dbe5b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101483 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
